.TH PHASORSTD_META 3 "August 2026" "Phasor 4.0.0" "Phasor Standard Library 4.0.0"
.SH NAME
phasorstd_meta \- Phasor runtime introspection, bytecode, and sub-interpreter functions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B include "std/meta.phs";
.PP
.B phs_version([type])
.B get_registers()
.B get_self()
.B load_bytecode(file_path)
.B save_bytecode(program, file_path)
.PP
.I "The following are unavailable in sandboxed builds:"
.B phs_op(opcode[, a[, b[, c]]])
.B self_stack_run(opcode, ...)
.B vm_push(value)
.B vm_pop()
.B new_vm()
.B free_vm(vm)
.B phs_compile(source, module_path, include_paths, defines)
.B phs_exec(vm, bytecode)
.B phs_eval(vm, source, module_path, include_paths, defines, verbose)
.fi
.SH TYPES
.TP
.B Bytecode
.RS
.nf
struct Bytecode {
    instructions: Instruction[],
    constants: any[],
    variables: any,
    scopeVarLists: ScopeVar[][],
    functionEntries: any,
    functionParamCounts: any,
    functionParamTypeNames: any,
    functionParamArrayDims: any,
    functionReturnTypeNames: any,
    functionReturnArrayDims: any,
    nextVarIndex: int,
    structs: StructInfo[],
    structEntries: any
}
.fi
.PP
Represents a fully compiled Phasor program: the flat instruction list, the constant pool, and
the metadata needed to resolve variables, functions, and struct layouts at run time. The
.BR functionEntries ", " functionParamCounts ", " functionParamTypeNames ", " functionParamArrayDims ,
.BR functionReturnTypeNames ", " functionReturnArrayDims ", and " structEntries
fields are internal lookup tables (typed as \fBany\fR, i.e.\& dictionary-like) rather than
plain arrays of records; treat them as opaque unless you are matching the runtime's own
bytecode-serialization format. Returned by \fBget_self()\fR and \fBload_bytecode()\fR, and
accepted by \fBsave_bytecode()\fR and \fBphs_exec()\fR.
.RE
.TP
.B Instruction
.RS
.nf
struct Instruction {
    op: int,
    o1: int,
    o2: int,
    o3: int
}
.fi
.PP
One entry in \fBBytecode.instructions\fR: an opcode number and up to three integer operands
(register indices, constant-pool indices, or jump targets, depending on the opcode).
.RE
.TP
.B ScopeVar
.RS
.nf
struct ScopeVar {
    index: int,
    name: string
}
.fi
.PP
Maps a variable's storage index to its source-level name within one lexical scope; a
\fBBytecode\fR has one array of these per scope in \fBscopeVarLists\fR.
.RE
.TP
.B StructInfo
.RS
.nf
struct StructInfo {
    name: string,
    firstConstIndex: int,
    fieldCount: int,
    fieldNames: string[],
    fieldArrayDims: int[][],
    fieldTypeNames: string[]
}
.fi
.PP
Describes one struct type declared in a compiled program: its name, its fields' names and
type names, the array dimensions of any array-typed fields, and where its field-name
constants begin in the constant pool.
.RE
.TP
.B VM
.RS
.nf
struct VM {
    handle: any
}
.fi
.PP
An opaque handle to an independent virtual machine instance, as created by \fBnew_vm()\fR.
The pre-built variable \fBNULL_VM\fR (\fBVM{handle: null}\fR) can be passed anywhere a
\fBVM\fR is expected to mean "run this on a disposable, throwaway VM instead of a specific
one" \(em see \fBphs_exec()\fR and \fBphs_eval()\fR.
.RE
.SH VERSION AND INTROSPECTION
.TP
.BR phs_version ([type])
Get the Phasor runtime's version.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBtype\fR 12
Optional integer selecting a single version component instead of the full version string:
\fB0\fR for major, \fB1\fR for minor, \fB2\fR for patch. The named constants
\fBsys_version_type.major\fR, \fBsys_version_type.minor\fR, and \fBsys_version_type.patch\fR
are available once \fBstd/meta.phs\fR is included.
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
With no arguments, the full version string (e.g.\& \fB"4.0.0"\fR). With \fBtype\fR given,
just that component as a string (e.g.\& \fB"4"\fR for major).
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if more than 1 argument is given
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR get_registers ()
Get a snapshot of every value currently held in the calling VM's registers.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
None
.PP
.B Returns:
Array of the current values of all VM registers, in register order. This is a low-level
debugging aid that reflects Phasor's register-based execution model.
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR get_self ()
Get the compiled bytecode of the currently running program.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
None
.PP
.B Returns:
A \fBBytecode\fR struct describing the currently executing program, suitable for saving with
\fBsave_bytecode()\fR or re-running with \fBphs_exec()\fR
.RE
.SH BYTECODE PERSISTENCE
.TP
.BR load_bytecode (file_path)
Load a previously saved bytecode file from disk.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBfile_path\fR 12
Path to a bytecode file previously written by \fBsave_bytecode()\fR
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
A \fBBytecode\fR struct
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if \fBfile_path\fR does not exist, or if the file cannot be parsed as bytecode
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR save_bytecode (program, file_path)
Serialize a \fBBytecode\fR struct to disk.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBprogram\fR 12
A \fBBytecode\fR struct, typically from \fBget_self()\fR or \fBphs_compile()\fR
.IP \fBfile_path\fR 12
Destination file path
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Boolean true on success, false on failure
.RE
.SH LOW-LEVEL VM ACCESS
These functions are compiled out of sandboxed builds.
.TP
.BR phs_op (opcode[, a[, b[, c]]])
Execute a single raw VM instruction directly.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBopcode\fR 12
The opcode to execute, as either its integer value or its mnemonic name (e.g.\&
\fB"ADD"\fR)
.IP "\fBa\fR, \fBb\fR, \fBc\fR" 12
Up to three optional integer operands for the instruction (default \fB0\fR if omitted)
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Integer result of executing the instruction
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if more than 4 arguments are given, or if \fBopcode\fR is not an integer or
string
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR self_stack_run (opcode, ...)
Push a set of values onto the VM's operand stack and then execute an opcode that consumes
them from the stack.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBopcode\fR 12
The opcode to execute, as either its integer value or its mnemonic name
.IP \fB...\fR 12
Any number of additional values, pushed onto the stack in reverse order (so the first extra
argument ends up on top of the stack) before \fBopcode\fR runs
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
The value popped off the stack after \fBopcode\fR executes
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if \fBopcode\fR is not an integer or string
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR vm_push (value)
Push a value onto the calling VM's operand stack.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBvalue\fR 12
Any value
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Null
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR vm_pop ()
Pop and return the top value from the calling VM's operand stack.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
None
.PP
.B Returns:
The value that was on top of the stack
.RE
.SH SUB-INTERPRETERS
These functions are compiled out of sandboxed builds. They let a script compile and run
Phasor source or bytecode in an independent virtual machine, separate from the one running
the calling script.
.TP
.BR new_vm ()
Create a new, independent virtual machine instance.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
None
.PP
.B Returns:
A \fBVM\fR handle. Must eventually be released with \fBfree_vm()\fR.
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR free_vm (vm)
Release a virtual machine instance created by \fBnew_vm()\fR.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBvm\fR 12
A \fBVM\fR handle from \fBnew_vm()\fR
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Boolean true if the handle was valid and freed, false otherwise
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR phs_compile (source, module_path, include_paths, defines)
Compile Phasor source code to bytecode without executing it.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBsource\fR 12
Phasor source code to compile
.IP \fBmodule_path\fR 12
Path used to resolve relative \fBinclude\fR statements and to attribute error messages to a
file; may be an empty string
.IP \fBinclude_paths\fR 12
Array of additional directories to search for included files, beyond the current directory,
\fBmodule_path\fR, and any paths from the \fBPHASOR_INCLUDE_PATH\fR environment variable
.IP \fBdefines\fR 12
Array of preprocessor defines, each either \fB"NAME"\fR (defined with value \fB1\fR) or
\fB"NAME=VALUE"\fR
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
A \fBBytecode\fR struct, suitable for \fBphs_exec()\fR or \fBsave_bytecode()\fR
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if compilation fails (lexer, parser, or codegen error)
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR phs_exec (vm, bytecode)
Run a compiled \fBBytecode\fR struct on a VM.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBvm\fR 12
A \fBVM\fR handle from \fBnew_vm()\fR, or \fBNULL_VM\fR to run on a disposable temporary VM
that is discarded when execution finishes
.IP \fBbytecode\fR 12
A \fBBytecode\fR struct, from \fBget_self()\fR, \fBload_bytecode()\fR, or
\fBphs_compile()\fR
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Integer result code from running the bytecode on the VM
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR phs_eval (vm, source, module_path, include_paths, defines, verbose)
Compile and immediately run Phasor source code on a VM, in one call.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBvm\fR 12
A \fBVM\fR handle from \fBnew_vm()\fR, or \fBNULL_VM\fR to run on a disposable temporary VM
that is discarded when execution finishes
.IP \fBsource\fR 12
Phasor source code to compile and run
.IP \fBmodule_path\fR 12
Same meaning as in \fBphs_compile()\fR
.IP \fBinclude_paths\fR 12
Same meaning as in \fBphs_compile()\fR
.IP \fBdefines\fR 12
Same meaning as in \fBphs_compile()\fR
.IP \fBverbose\fR 12
Boolean; if true, the parsed syntax tree is printed before execution, for debugging
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Integer result code from running the compiled source on the VM
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if compilation fails
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.B Version and Bytecode Introspection
.PP
.nf
.RS
include "std/meta.phs";

puts(phs_version());                          // e.g. "4.0.0"
puts(phs_version(sys_version_type.major));    // e.g. "4"

var bc = get_self();
putf("This program has %d instructions", bc.instructions.length);

save_bytecode(bc, "snapshot.phsb");
var reloaded = load_bytecode("snapshot.phsb");
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B Running an Independent Sub-Interpreter
.PP
.nf
.RS
include "std/meta.phs";

var vm = new_vm();
var result = phs_eval(
    vm,
    "print \e"hello from a sub-interpreter\\n\e"; return 42;",
    "",
    [],
    [],
    false
);
putf("Sub-interpreter returned: %d", result);
free_vm(vm);

// Or use NULL_VM for a one-off, throwaway run
var quick = phs_eval(NULL_VM, "return 1 + 1;", "", [], [], false);

// Compile once, execute (potentially many times) later
var compiled = phs_compile("return 7 * 6;", "", [], []);
var vm2 = new_vm();
var answer = phs_exec(vm2, compiled);
free_vm(vm2);
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B Low-Level VM Access
.PP
.nf
.RS
include "std/meta.phs";

vm_push(10);
vm_push(20);
var sum = self_stack_run("ADD", 5, 7);  // pushes 7 then 5, then runs ADD
.RE
.fi
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
.BR phs_op() ", " self_stack_run() ", " vm_push() ", " vm_pop() ", " new_vm() ", " free_vm() ,
.BR phs_compile() ", " phs_exec() ", and " phs_eval()
are all unavailable in sandboxed builds; scripts that must run in both sandboxed and
non-sandboxed environments should guard their use with \fBis_sandboxed()\fR (see
\fBphasorstd_sys\fR(3))
.IP \(bu 2
Struct and type introspection helpers \(em
.BR get_elements() ", " get_elements_values() ", and " get_type()
\(em live in the
.B type
module, not here; see \fBphasorstd_type\fR(3)
.IP \(bu 2
Passing \fBNULL_VM\fR to \fBphs_exec()\fR or \fBphs_eval()\fR runs the given bytecode or
source on a brand-new VM that is discarded immediately afterward; there is no way to inspect
its state once the call returns
.IP \(bu 2
Every \fBVM\fR returned by \fBnew_vm()\fR should be released with exactly one call to
\fBfree_vm()\fR once it is no longer needed
.IP \(bu 2
Most \fBBytecode\fR fields beyond \fBinstructions\fR, \fBconstants\fR, \fBstructs\fR, and
\fBscopeVarLists\fR are internal, dictionary-like (\fBany\fR-typed) lookup tables rather than
typed arrays; treat them as implementation detail unless working directly with the runtime's
bytecode format
